In 2005 when 'Writing the TV Drama Series first came out, é greeted 
with wonderful reviews and welcomed by professionals ¢ § 
industry — the kinds of responses any author would hope to receive. I’m 
grateful for all that support. So¦ book was adopted by writing classes 
at film schools and by some mentoring programs at TV ê, and had 
begun to sell around ă. 
So if it ain’t broke, why fix it? 
Because we’re ¢ heart of a dynamically evolving art and business, and 
like Alice in Wonderland, we º run very fast just to stay in Ã 
place. 
This volatility õ only about new shows, though I will discuss some 
interesting series that weren’t in ¿ edition including Lost, Grey’s 
Anatomy, Battlestar Galactica, CSI: Miami, House, and others. But so- 
called “hot” shows come and go (sometimes very quickly!), and learning 
to be an effective § writer isn’t about chasing each season’s hits. 
ü last two years, new industry-wide patterns and opportunities have 
appeared which Çn impact on writers who are coming ï busi¬ 
ness. For example, original ®s by novices are being sought by agents 
and ã, so I’ve added a section on writing ư ®. 
Mobisodes and webisodes not only interface with current series, but 
also open fresh creative prospects for writers. So I’ve added material on 
“eTV” (enhanced TV) and other emerging outlets to a final chapter, “In 
the Future.” 
Kinds of storytelling are in flux too. At Ã time as venues open, 
ê are scrambling to adjust, some changing À traditional 
four-act structure to five or even six acts. Strongly serialized ³s, like 
24, Lost, ¬ great shows on HBO proved that “long narratives” 
would draw and hold devoted viewers. But they also spawned a number 
of network serials that didn’t succeed, while procedurals like CSI (in all 
xiii 
© TV 0 R R M H 5 T H I E S / P R M 0 0 U G L R S X I V 
its incarnations) have continued to top the ratings. So ţ edition I’ve 
added a section on writing procedurals. 
Meanwhile, increasing fragmentation of č on specialized cable 
channels has enabled niche programming that would never have made 
the cut ¢ era of three ê. That narrow-casting is producing 
adventurous, high-quality products like Battlestar Galactica (¦ SciFi 
Channel), The Shield (on FX), and Dexter (on Showtime). 
If opportunities seemed promising when ¿ edition was published, 
they’re blossoming now. It’s a great time ½ for §. 
More than a thousand students have come through my classes ¢ 
two decades I’ve taught ª U.S.C. School of Cinema-Television (now 
re-named the USC School of Cinematic Arts). At Ã time, my profes¬ 
sional career was growing to include story editor and producer credits 
on § ³s, the Humanitas Prize, and awards and nominations 
including Emmys, Writers Guild, and American Women in Radio and 
Television, and a position ¦ Board of Directors ¡ Writers Guild. 
Always, my screenwriting and teaching have complemented each other. 
I bring my working life ï classroom: What is it like to be actively 
breaking stories, writing, rewriting, giving and getting notes, seeing how 
your scripts translate to screen? I clearly recall freelancing § 
°, but I also know how the other side ¡ desk reacts to pitches 
because I’ve been in both chairs. I’ve written for ê, cable, syndica¬ 
tion, public TV, primetime and off-prime, and for virtually every ­ 
genre. In my class, students learn from someone who has been there. 
Imagine you’ve stopped by a typical episodic ³ class ¢ middle 
of a term. Ten students sit at a table, scripts and DVDs from our in-class 
library — House, Big Love, Lost, Battlestar Galactica, Grey’s Anatomy, 
The Sopranos, Nip/Tuck, C.S.I. — among others ¢ middle. The class 
has not begun, but people are returning scripts and videos, reaching for 
one they want next. Then we start. 
Someone asks why Đ was left hanging at ² ę she 
saw, and I discuss the 22-hour “long narrative” of a season, or how char¬ 
acter arcs cross multiple °, or stories that are closed on one level 
but not another. 
Now we’re warmed up. The class operates like the writing staff of a TV 
series in which writers suggest improvements on each other’s work, while I 
function as “show-runner,” or final authority on revisions. The standard is 
professional quality, and our models are ū brilliant, incisive, daring 
scripts on ê or cable. Since I hold the bar so high, no one brings in 
trivial subjects. Because § ³ frequently reflects complex and 
wrenching problems in contemporary society — such as racism, sexism. 
1 
2 W fl I I I N 6 THE IV D R R M fi 5 E R I E 5 / P R M D 0 U 6 L R S 
violence, spirituality, and sexual identity — the writers must confront 
these issues with honesty at Ã time as they learn their craft. 
On other occasions, I come to class ¢ midst of my writing, full of 
some creative problem, and let the class in on my process. Or I might 
screen a clip and deconstruct its elements and discuss how it fits within 
the total structure of í. All this leads to finished scripts that aim to 
be competitive µ best ³ written for screen. 
That’s my goal for you too as you work with this book. My students are 
sophisticated, smart, dedicated, and some are wonderfully talented. Still, I 
was initially surprised at whªy didn’t know. When I add my summer 
seminars which are open ¥ public, I discovered strange misconcep¬ 
tions about §, the art and craft of TV ³, ¬ life of a 
working writer. That’s è my reasons for creating this book, since I’m 
uniquely aware of what beginning writers want and ä learn. 
My approach is practical: The better you write the more work you’ll get. 
Give ’em better than they deserve because you deserve the best, even àr 
first assignment is on something you leave off your resume years later. 
As you go through this book, imagine you’re in my classroom. I’ll be 
talking to you, even asking you questions. Though I can’t hear your 
answers, I suggest you treª chapters interactively anyway so you’ll be 
applying the principles. 
Between chapters and in “Spotlights” I’ve invited illustrious “guest speakers” 
to address you. We’re proud to host: John Wells (£R, The West Wing), 
David Milch (Deadwood, NYPD Blue), Steven Bochco {Hill Street Blues, 
NYPD Blue), Ann Donahue (CSI), Melissa Rosenberg [Dexter), Georgia 
Jeffries [China Beach and ®s), Damon Lindelof [Lost), Ron Moore 
[Battlestar Galactica) and other insightful § writer-ã. 
Towards Ð, we follow a group of former students from graduation 
through breaking ï business and launching careers as § 
writers. It’s an opportunity to learn from writers ¢ trenches. 
And the final chapter takes you ï future Ú discussion of 
emerging venues and possibilities that await new writers. 
Throughout this book, you’ll find useful tools, a complete map ¡ TV 
series terrain, and lessons from those who have traveled it before. But, 
ultimately, ú ½ better is to be true to what’s real ř 
stories and ½ more. You’re not alone — I’ll show you how. 
From 
John Wellj 
John Wells is Executive Producer of ER, The West Wing, Third Watch, 
and past president ¡ Writers Guild of America, West. 
PD: æ could go back in time and talk to ư young self when 
you were a student in film school or college, what ó know now 
that you wish you’d known then about writing and producing § 
series? 
JW: I wish Fd known how long é ¶ take. You come out and 
you sort of assume it’s ¶ be a couple-of-years process ń don’t 
really start making any headway until you’ve written about a foot and a 
half of material, measured up off the floor. That’s ầ really start to 
think ûself as ą in ú you look at ă. It’s a craft 
that takes a tremendous amount of time. 
I wish I had more of a sense that é much more like learning to play 
a musical instrument. After four or five years you start to not embarrass 
yourself. It takes 10 years before ¾ even begin to call yourself profi¬ 
cient. And that’s very difficult for students because they’ve been through 
12 years of primary school, four years of college, and often a couple of 
years of graduate school ¬y think they’ve already done 16, 18 years 
of education, so they ¹ go do it right now, though they’ve actually 
just started. 
It looks deceptively easy À outside. æ look ª lowest 
common denominator you think “I can do that.” The craft that’s neces¬ 
sary — the time it takes ō å trial and error and to keep going 
with it — that takes a very long time to develop. I’m very suspicious of 
writers who haven’t been writing for 10 years. I will often ask people for 
3 
4 « 0 fl fl É / P R M 0 0 U 6 L fl S 
three or four or five pieces of material if I’ve read one thing ¡irs that 
I like. I know they’ve given me the thing they’re proudest of, and I’m 
looking ī the growth, and how much they’ve done and how much 
they’ve committed themselves ¥ long-term process of writing. 
I’ve supervised well over 600 scripts, and personally written well over 
100, and I still finish each one disappointed in my work. It’s a life-long 
endeavor, never something you succeed at. I’ve been working profession¬ 
ally for 20 years and I’m always learning something new every day about 
writing. 
PD: You could have chosen ½ in any medium. Why did you choose 
§? 
JW: The feature world, which I remain involved in, õ a medium, 
generally, where you’re able ½ about character ¢ depth I like to 
write about character. There are characters now on ER whose growth I’ve 
been writing about for years. I don’t mean to compare myself to Dickens, 
but I heard Steven Bochco talk about that years ago, when he explained 
that what he was İ do on Hill Street was like ú Dickens 
published a chapter a week. 
And subject matter is different in §. The kinds of things we can 
write about seriously are more appealing than most of ů’re offered 
to do in features. 
Beyond that, it’s much easier to be involved creatively ř work in 
§ than in feature films. It happens a lot faster, so there’s not time 
for as many cooks ¢ kitchen. But also you get ī your work and 
see it quickly. I’ve done work on features that haven’t been produced for 
years, and [when asked for another draft] it becomes hard to remember 
ů had in mind ầ first wrote it three years ago. In televi¬ 
sion, you’ll finish ś and see dailies on it 10 days later. 
PD: People talk about how § is changing now with cable and 
the influence of TiVo. What does the future hold ± art of § 
³? 
JW: The technology makes for short-term changes, but we’re still doing 
what Chaucer was doing a thousand years ago. We’re still writing stories. 
I think we are structured in such a way that we’re interested in people, and 
we’re interested in hearing their stories and metaphors for our own lives 
and going through cathartic experiences. That hasn’t changed. 
FROM JOHN WELLS 5 
I actually think it’s a more exciting time for ą because there are 
many more ways for your material × made. You can write something 
and make it on a digital videocam that you buy at a store. You Çn 
opportunity ť on shows on cable which have content ¾’t 
do on broadcast §. The opportunities are limitless. There isn’t as 
