Imagine the power. 
Picture the whole world dotted with hundreds of millions of homes glowing 
µ blue light of TV sets. Inside each home — ư home, for 
example — visitors tell stories áir dreams and problems, loves 
and rages, their thrills ¬ir losses. You care ám, probably 
more than you admit; you even talk ám when they’re not around 
— after all, they come every week. Sometimes they’re broiling over issues 
¢ news. Or sick and scared about that, or lying, or brave. At one time 
they were attacked and fought back and barely survived. But no matter 
what, they’ll be back next week, your same friends, there with you ř 
most vulnerable place, at home ầ’re tired after work. Intimate. 
Maybe Óm is Tony Soprano, the mob boss, asking Uncle Junior, 
“I thought you loved me,” and watching Junior’s lip quiver, unable to 
answer. Or Detective Andy ¤ of NYPD Blue, just reconciled with 
his adult son, lifting the sheet off a corpse, to discover his son. On West 
Wing, you were surprised by a swell of pride when reason triumphed 
in a hard-won Supreme Court appointment. You were drawn ï 
D.A.’s moral quandary when an old man can’t be stopped from confessing 
revenge against a swindler who took his house, on Law and Order. And 
you couldn’t resist rooting ± nurses on ER ¢ midst of tragedies 
as they dealt Ún irascible patient by pushing his gurney ¢ hall and 
“taking his temperature” Ú plastic flower in his rear. Joy and tears up 
close and personal. 
