Among the traits that distinguish primetime series (both ³s and 
comedies) from other kinds of screenwriting, three are especially signifi¬ 
cant for writers: endless character arcs, the “long narrative” for serials, 
¬ collaborative process. 
EPISODIC CHARACTERIZATION 
In feature writing you were probably told to create an arc for your protag¬ 
onist that takes him from one state to its opposite; the character struggles 
towards a goal and once that is attained, your story ends. Someone who is 
unable to love is changed when a mate/child/friend appears and, through 
fighting the relationship, the character is finally able to love. Or someone 
who has been wronged seeks revenge and either achieves it or dies ± 
cause. All fine for movies that end. But series don’t. 
So how ó progress a narrative without an arc? Well, you create a 
different ô arc. Remember what I said about series characters being 
more like people you know than figures in a plot. ær friend has an 
extreme experience, you continue knowing him after the event. You’re 
invested ¢ process, not just the outcome. 
But watch out — this does not mean đ are flat. Your continuing 
cast should never be mere witnesses ¥ challenge ¡ week. On the 
contrary, characters who are not transformed Ü plot need something 
instead: dimension. Think of it like this: instead of developing horizon¬ 
tally towards a goal, the character develops vertically, exploring internal 
