the page. Ö represented by an agent or manager, of course, the 
cover is done Üir office ńr agent ơ the contact. 
Do register the completed format µ Writers Guild (specifics on that 
are ¢ Appendix). But do not put your WGA registration number on 
the cover — it’s tacky. Also don’t include any dates or draft numbers. 
Every draft you deliver is ¿, untrammeled and never before revealed 
to human eyes — or that’s ů’d like the producer to think. (No one 
wants something that’s been rejected or gathering dust.) 
On the top of page one, write a “Log Line.” You’ve encountered that term 
in screenwriting classes, but did you know it originated in §.^ For 
decades, § station owners have been required Ü FCC to keep 
a log of everything they broadcast. These ź fit on a line, like “Lassie 
finds lost boy.” Then TV Guide and newspapers began printing short 
episode summaries like this one from Joan of Arcadia: “Joan learns the 
downside of vanity when God asks her to take a cosmetics class.” 
So¦ promotional tag found its ě movie posters, as in: “Tom 
Cruise stars as Nathan Algren, a heroic American military officer hired 
Ü Emperor of Japan to tra¢ country’s first army. After being 
captured by his Samurai enemies, Algren becomes unexpectedly inspired 
Üir way of life and fights to defend what he has come to love.” Or, 
for a simpler example: “The women of Stepford Ç secret.” Before 
long, either full log lines (like the one from The Last Samurai) or “hooks” 
(like the one from The Stepford Wives) became necessary to pitch films, 
°, and series, not merely to log them or advertise. 
« 0 R fl É / P R M D 0 U e I H S 3 8 
A log line for ë ò less specific than ð summaries you’ll 
use for individual °. The goal is to orient a listener (yes, listener, not 
reader) to your project, to catch an executive’s attention. “MTV Cops” is 
a famous log line for Miami Vice țn era when MTV was new and 
hot. When he was first presenting The O.C., Josh Schwartz knew the Fox 
network was looking for an updated Beverly Hills 90210, so he pitched 
his show as “90210 ¦ beach in Orange County,” and later admitted 
that was a Trojan horse to set up a far more nuanced show. 
Once your log line sizzles, take ¿ couple of pages û format for 
an Overview. This õ a summary of ā (a common mistake) but 
an introduction to ă ¬ quest ¡ whole series, including 
location, style, tone, context, and, most of all, characters. Though full 
characterizations come later, the main cast must be mentioned up front. 
Use brief tags like “a single, middle-aged probation officer who adopts a 
child from è her cases” (from Allison Anders’ series proposal ü 
Echo)-, “a 29-year-old Congressional aide running against her boss” (from 
Rod Lurie’s proposed The Capital City). 
Withţ Overview, suggest springboards for future ° so deci¬ 
sion-makers believe â has legs. That is, state the source of future 
°, for example: Each week Joan gets a new challenge from God; 
each week the detectives pursue three cases, walking a thin line between 
vigilante justice ¬ job. As in any fiction writing, make ‘em laugh, 
cry, be scared or angry or fall in love. The overview ò as far as you 
get in a pitch, so make it soar. 
Follow the Overview µ centerpiece of any series: characters. If viewers 
don’t root for your main cast, if they’re not compelled to find out how the 
people are coping or loving or fighting back each week, you don’t have 
anything. Remember, TV ³ isn’t really á concept; it runs ¦ 
emotional fuel of endless character arcs, as discussed in Chapter One. 
Take one page each ± few leading roles. I said few. Yes, you’ve seen 
excellent ensemble shows with casts in double-digits, but in a proposal, 
the listener’s eyes will glaze over after you get past your third or fourth 
character. So focus on one fascinating, eminently castable character and 
us in her spirit and goals. You can do that again with roles for 
antagonists or partners, providing their connections ¥ protagonist are 
gripping. Beyond those few, summarize the secondary cast with only a tag 
for each, even if those parts will grow later. 
