I use the two-page scene as a target for students partly because inexperi¬ 
enced writers have difficulty accomplishing a complete ­ beat in 
fewer pages, and when they write longer than two pages, their scenes tend 
to lose focus or become redundant. 
HOW fl C L fl 5 5 I C 5 C R I P T IS C R fl F I [ D 7 7 
Historically, screen scenes used to be long, more like stage plays. æ 
look at great movies À 1940s, like the ones starring Humphrey 
Bogart, you’ll see some scenes that run five or even seven pages. They 
reflect a different era where the slow evolvement of a ­ moment, 
gradually experienced, was part ¡ pace of life. Whether the change 
was caused Ü 15-second information module of Sesame Street, or TV 
ads where a one-minute commercial is long and 30 seconds is normal, or 
the speed of computers, where you’re growling ª screen if a function 
takes two seconds — an electronics-savvy populace is quickly bored. If 
ç attention drifts, or is way ahead of where you’re going µ 
story, you’ve lost your moment. On § today, a one-minute beat is 
more welcome than a three-minute scene, and » starting page four 
of a single encounter, that’s a red flag. 
Notice also that I’m using the words “minute” and “page” interchange¬ 
ably. That’s just shorthand. A minute per page ò an average, but it’s 
not always accurate. Pages of dialogue move faster, while action eats up 
lots of time. As Úll my construction advice. I’m pointing out a general 
design, not policing whether you color outside the lines! 
