The excerpts you 11 read ţ chapter are from í that opens Ú 
“teaser,” though not all series begin with one. A teaser, also called “a cold 
opening, refers to ­ material before the titles (before the name of 
â and credits). It ò a one-minute “hook,” or as long as ten 
minutes that includes several small scenes, making it nearly as full as a 
traditional whole act. In any style, it exists to grab viewers faster than the 
enemy, which Î remote. The notion is to open the hour Ún action, 
image, situation or character that provokes å anticipation to keep 
viewers through the title sequence and into ¿ act. 
HOW H C L H n I C 5 C R I P r IS C R H F T E 0 7 9 
However, increasingly, title sequences along µme music have been 
dumped by ê preferring to grab viewers with uninterrupted ³. 
The “tease” is ð itself, and titles scroll over Act One. 
Often, a teaser sets out the problem of ę. For example, on a 
detective show, the crime to be solved ò enacted or discovered in 
the teaser. But even with¢ detective franchise, each ş unique. 
Looking back ª classic Miami Vice series, the lush teasers featured 
life in Miami and a provocative crime in progress in a textured envi¬ 
ronment usually involving drugs. The main cast never appeared ¢ 
teaser. Similarly, in The X-Files, a mysterious phenomenon occupied the 
cinematic teaser, though Mulder and Scully didn’t learn about it until they 
were contacted ¢ir office in Act One. In contrast, both Law and Order 
(classic) and NYPD Blue usually opened µ detectives arriving at a 
crime scene after the deed is done. 
In many law and medical shows, a case arrives and incites the events of 
the episode. But in ER and Grey’s Anatomy, where emphasis is ¦ 
ensemble cast and ° usually cover a single day, teasers have some¬ 
times shown the continuing characters waking, getting ready for work, 
or traveling ť, launching their personal stories that will spin out in 
the episode. 
HBO’s Six Feet Under always opened with someone dying (as bizarrely 
as possible) who turned up ¢ Fisher family funeral home in Act One, 
underlining ·’s notion that life is random. But that teaser was 
rarely a component ¡ episode’s ³; its link Îmatic. The prison 
³ OZ, which used to run on HBO, also opened µme rather 
than continuing story — a philosophical monologue, in fact. Even the 
classic sitcom Cheers was an example of a teaser that reminded viewers 
of ·’s world (a neighborhood bar where “everybody knows your 
name”), instead of beginning a ­ arc. 
Ö a new writer, though, I recommend sticking with story rather 
than message. A swiftly propelled ­ tension will lure a reader to 
turn the page and a viewer ī what happens next. Real storytelling is 
difficult, and I’ve seen too many students fool themselves into thinking 
they can impress by opening Ú weighty theme or abstruse philosophy, 
when they’re actually avoiding the challenge of impelling an arc. The best 
teasers tend to be the best ³. 
