M[m Before we get into content, notice that a TV dramś looks just like a 
theatrical screenplay. This ý news à’ve written sitcoms because 
half-hour comedies use a unique form. Most screenwriting software will 
give you format options, so choose “standard,” “screenplay,” or what¬ 
ever term your system uses for writing movies. And, of course, you know 
better than to use a divided page or anything called “video.” 
Now, I said this show starts Ú teaser which precedes Act One, yet 
you see no heading. Some series do write TEASER ¢ beginning, then 
they EADE OUT at ² the teaser and start a new page headed ACT 
ONE. Other shows start with ACT ONE ª top of page one, whether 
or not there’s a teaser. The only ě know what a particular show does 
is check out Óir sample scripts. In any case. Acts Two, Three and 
Eour ơgin on new pages and be headed ACT TWO and so forth, 
centered ª top. 
OPENING SCENES 
Look at #1 which simply establishes the Precinct building. Is that a scene.’ 
No! Remember, I told you a scene is a ­ beat, not a production 
location. This ô shot merely tells viewers where we are. 
#2 opens the action Ú personal detail that speaks volumes without 
dialogue. Think about what is revealed when a man tries on reading 
glasses for ¿ time. What ó suppose is ¢ character’s mind? 
Notice how he hides them the moment ¼ enters. In Chapter One, 
I spoke á essential intimacy of §, and here it is: In his 
private moment, ¤ is self-conscious about aging. Insecurity that 
his eyesight is weakening gives viewers a window to his ä prove his 
status ţ episode. 
The ¼ character is played by a younger man — tall, handsome 
Jimmy Smits. In comparison with ¤ — fat, balding and now 
needing glasses — the rivalry is set in motion Àir first appearance. 
The script doesn’t spell out how each man looks — that’s not necessary in 
a TV series where everyone working on · knows who the actors 
are. In fact, character tags, which are common in features, are slight, if 
they’re included here at all. Still, everything ű emotionally is ¦ 
page ţ tiny moment. 
« 0 R H M R ¨ fl M 0 D U G E R S 9 B 
Notice also that dialogue begins on page one. That’s normal for §, 
though š have read features (especially older ones) where descrip¬ 
tion, atmosphere, and action without speech occupy ¿ few pages. 
Look ª ô dialogue on ¿ page, though. It seems like they’re 
saying nothing except hello. That’s not really what’s going on, though, 
is it? You ¹ aim for dialogue that is both natural and withheld. 
In other words, let viewers sense the meaning under the surface without 
hitting it ¦ head, as much as ¾. 
You could analyze that ¿ scene of this teaser ends ª bottom of 
page one when ¼ crosses off the name of ¤’s former partner 
and writes his own. It’s a gesture of power in a beat that’s all about rela¬ 
tive power. But to underst¬ ­ flow. I’d rather turn the page 
because Ã tension continues ï outer rooms. 
From having “lost” the unspoken battle with ¼ (which is only in 
his own mind), ¤ barrels out to his boss, Lt. Fancy in #4, and 
complains á new guy. From a ­ point of view, this page 
could be interpreted as a whole scene too because it has conflict between 
