Mms Let’s start by naming ż. “A” is about ¼ dying; “B” is 
¤ and his ex-wife Katie; “C” is Medavoy and Wolff. I’ve used 
simple tags, and that’s fine for identification, but ầ plan ư 
script, I suggest you create real ­ log lines. That way, you’ll be 
sure your stories have conflict - ñy’re actually stories, not just situ¬ 
ations. Using the example episode, š phrase log lines something 
like this: 
A: As ¼’s death becomes imminent, he fights until he ultimately 
makes peace with his memories. Withţ arena, Russell, ¤ and 
others have story arcs from denial and anger to acceptance. 
B: When ¤ is challenged by Katie’s drinking and desperation, he 
must overcome his anger, guilt and his own alcoholic history to be able to 
help her. 
C: Medavoy struggles to stay professional while an arrogant man’s 
complaints escalate. 
Notice in each case I’ve stated ż as issues for main cast, not the 
guest cast, though the guests (Katie and Wolff) are bringing the inciting 
incidents. 
COLD OPENING 
This teaser is entirely “A” story (slug lines #1 through #7), though it’s 
comprised of several beats. In planning an important opening like this, I 
șgin by making a mini-outline, planning each beat to build suspense. 
See if ¾ make ư list ¡ turning points m these first pages 
that end with “SMASH CUT TO,” which signals ² the teaser. 
Did you get these beats? 
• The Detectives spar with each other in anticipation; 
• The first reveal: Russell discovers the drainage; 
• ¤ schemes to find out what’s happening; 
• Tension mounts when Dr. Swan confirms the problem; 
• ¤ discovers it; we see his personal stakes; 
HOW fl CLASSIC SCRIPT IS C R fl f T E 0 
• ¤ and Russell reveal the crisis ¥ detectives, who leave, appre¬ 
hensive. 
Those six beats ramp quickly ï ³ using a basic storytelling 
progression: Anticipation — Expectation — Surprise. That is, viewers are 
led to anticipate an event, which holds people through the set-up. As the 
action advances, we come to expect certain outcomes. Instead, ð 
turns (twists), generating a sense of surprise which then begins a new 
sequence of suspense. You can readily create tension on screen that way, 
as this teaser demonstrates so well. 
Also, consider the use of humor ţ heavy episode. Here’s another age- 
old practice: increase the impact of tragedy by setting it against a comic 
foil. Shakespeare played his fools and foolishness — sometimes for belly 
laughs from č of his day - in his most tragic plays. Here, the 
episode begins Ú moment so light it’s almost silly — jockeying for 
position at a window. Then, as the ³ moves into Act One, an outra¬ 
geous fool appears ¢ person of Mr. Wolff. With all that in mind, let’s 
get into ¿ Act. 
Act One 
The “B” story about ¤ and Katie (#8 to #12) begins with several 
beats that are emotionally challenging to ¤ ¢ midst ¡ 
already-heartbreaking news his partner isn’t getting better. I just spoke 
á value of comic relief, yet here’s more stress. So why ó 
think the writers chose to come out ¡ title sequence and open Act 
One with this? 
Here are some possibilities: While the ¼ illness is suspenseful, it 
does not involve action for our continuing cast — except, of course, 
¼ and Russell, though even ±m, the conflicts are internal, and 
their decisions result mainly from reflection. In dealing with Katie’s very 
tangible distress, followed by her wandering off, ¤ is able to vent 
the claustrophobic hospital. 
Katie’s story also provides an opportunity for ¤ to externalize his 
feelings about ¼ (including his anger), without which he’d be stuck 
brooding. Throughout NYPD Blue, a prominent quality of ¤ as a 
character is his determination not to yield to helplessness even ¢ face 
¡ dregs of society, or, ţ case, death. Often, ¤ fails, but 
