here, Katie’s needs provide a way for him to be the rescuer when he cannot 
rescue ¼. 
See if ¾ discover other reasons for choosing this “B” story ţ 
episode, and why it’s an effective opening for Act One. 
Throughout this Act, take note ¡ fluid blÑ scenes with¢ 
Squad Room. In ¿ sample script, the scenes tended to be easily 
separated. Here, you’ll find multiple scenes in Ã time and place, 
choreographed directorially. For example, at ² #9, while ¤ 
takes Katie ï hallway, we see Medavoy and Martinez ascending the 
stairs. The point is that we don’t lose the persistence ¡ other char¬ 
acters and dilemmas. The action directs the camera to “Follow” them, 
moving our attention with this scene, but we’re also aware of material 
which is concurrent ¢ room we’ve left. 
Similarly, the “C” story with Wolff commences as ¤ goes towards 
his desk in #12. In fact, notice ñ “B” story is literally interspersed 
µ “C” story in #13. Towards ² #13, you’ll see multiple 
visual layers: While Medavoy and Wolff move to Medavoy’s desk, they pass 
¤ at his desk, and while ¤ speaks, we see Katie sneaking 
away ¢ background, followed by ¤ crossing the room, leaving 
us holding on Medavoy and Wolff. It’s a complex, but elegant ě 
create depth (multiple levels) on screen. 
This layering style — scene on scene in Ã time and place — is 
found in many other sophisticated series too, so watch for it. In writing 
ư script, though, I suggest you separate ż ầ 
plan them initially, and weave them together only ầ’re ready to 
go from outline to teleplay. (You can read more about this technique in 
Chapter Four.) 
