Many (though not all) dramë use parallel non-sequential stories 
denoted by letters A, B, C, and so forth. Three stories per episode are 
typical, though some shows have more. Each story is usually “driven by” 
one character ¢ main cast. 
Scenes tend to run two minutes or less. Six or seven scenes comprise an 
act in a classic network ³. With four acts, that adds to around 28 
scenes total in an hour episode. However, certain shows that have quick 
dialogue-intensive scenes have more beats per act; shows Úction 
sequences might have fewer. Shows with five or six acts may Çs few 
as 20 scenes total. 
A “teaser” is a prologue to ę and may incite one or more ¡ 
stories. 
By analyzing a quality show using the “grid,” ¾ see its structure at 
a glance. 
The best § shows demonstrate the principles of ­ art that 
apply to all quality screenwriting. 
