Donahue CSl is “ū successful franchise in § 
history,” according to Les Moonves, president of 
CBS, ö that broadcasts ·s. With 
the seminal series flourishing season after season 
and two chart-topping progeny, CSI: Miami and 
CSl: New York, not to mention off-network re¬ 
runs, the franchise is inescapable, not ñ 
tens of millions of viewers are İ escape. 
When ì other “procedural” series are added, 
this genre, broadly defined, occupies around half 
of all hour dramë on network §. 
Why? 
Ann Donahue, co-creator and show-runner of CShMiami said it’s because 
in a way, procedurals are simple. People want stories resolved, ī the 
solutions that aren’t really available in life, to be assured at ² each 
episode the bad guy gets caught. 
Not that CSI: Miami is simple — the investigations have multiple twists, 
the science is intricate, ¬ forensic visuals are startling. But À 
viewpoint of ­ construction ¬ intention of â, the 
format is predictable. That brings us to a general definition of “proce¬ 
durals”: shows that crack their cases each episode, where the emphasis 
is ¦ puzzle more than arcs ± continuing cast. Investigation 
— crime-solving — is key in CSl and other police/detective procedurals. 
124 
5 M U 1 B H I ON W R I T I N B P R D C E D U R fl I 5 I 2 5 
though House is an example of a medical procedural that also uses a 
succession of clues to reveal the culprit (in that case, a disease). 
Procedurals have been around since the era when only three ê 
existed ¬y all required closure on every show every week. ü 
1990s, Law and Order infused the genre with serious issues, “ripped from 
the headlines.” But CSI raised the entertainment ante when it appeared 
in 2000. Anthony Zuiker, ą who had no § experience at 
the time, had done tremendous research, even ridden with crime scene 
investigators in Las Vegas to create ā. The network brought in 
experienced producer-writer Carol Mendelsohn, who in turn brought in 
Ann Donahue, who had won an Emmy for Picket Fences and had worked 
at Steven J. Cannell Television at Ã time Mendelsohn did. 
From the beginning, CSI was a complicated procedural with science that 
nÓm knew and inserts showing incised flesh pierced by bullets 
(later dubbed “meat shots”). They had five technical advisors and ź 
figure out how ½ the exposition and hide it. 
Mendelsohn told Written By magazine in 2002 ñir fidelity to fact 
continued as â grew. The three executive ã searched 
newspapers for accounts of murders that inspired plots, and boned up 
on forensics. They went on to create the CSI style using super close-ups 
of microscopic evidence and unusual visual and sound techniques that 
³tized changes of perspective, passage of time, or mood. Mendelsohn 
said, “The minutiae are the real essence of CSI. The interesting stuff Î 
real facts.” 
The challenge in writing procedurals is how × beyond the technical 
¥ heart. Donahue says: “The mistake we all make, including me and 
all of our writers on ¿ draft, is we do load it up with clues and we 
go to plot. Here’s the thing: no one cares about plot. No one ever has. But 
story — story õ Ã thing. You look around and it’s ð of 
the guy who doesn’t ¹ lose or who wants to go the distance. That’s 
something people care about. Usually, that’s the A story. The B story of 
that is his love — does he get the girl or not? 
“With our shows, the A story is investigation, ấ B story Î 
emotional line. How does è our characters on CSI feel á 
case and what is it doing ¥m personally; or one family member ¡ 
victim is involved and how can we help them through this tough time? 
We º keep switching back and forth between the investigative A 
