IH[ Outline 
The first step of writing involves listing the scenes ř script. This 
process has several names in addition to “!@#$%'^&’'''()+,” which is what 
š ¹ call it when wrestling down the structure. Actual 
names include “outline,” “step outline,” “beat outline,” “beat sheet,” 
and “treatment,” ấy all amount to figuring out the order of events 
ř teleplay. 
“Treatment” has a specific definition, usually observed only ¢ breach, 
and it’s not a word you’ll hear much in §. Technically, a treat¬ 
ment ș a half to two-thirds the length of a finished script written 
in prose (not screenplay form) that includes every scene ¢ order it 
occurs, as well as tone, style, and descriptions, and is virtually the entire 
script lacking only dialogue. Believe me, nobody writes treatments like 
that. If someone asks you for a “treatment” û story whªy really 
mean is a few pages that summarize it. Even for full-length features. I’ve 
heard the term “treatment” apply to a three-page pitch. Don’t bother with 
it unless you’re asked. 
A “beat sheet,” as used by some ã, is mid way between a treat¬ 
ment and an outline. It parses your show into acts and describes the big 
story elements, though it doesn’t spell out specific scenes. So we’re talking 
about seeing the ­ turns, not a diagram for writing. 
“Beat outline” is sometimes used interchangeably with “outline” because 
it’s a list of what’s actually written ¢ teleplay, and usually those “beats” 
are scenes. On one show where I worked, the ã asked writers for 
a very full outline (like a treatment with numbers), but when ć met, 
they would reduce the narrative ¢ outline to a more skeletal list they 
called a beat outline so the construction could be analyzed. 
For example, an outline scene might say something like “While Jane clings 
¥ ledge, frozen in fear, desperately İ convince Portia to release 
Sally, Portia taunts Jane to climb out × her.” The simplified beat might 
say “Jane on ledge — Portia: “Come get her.” It’s an internal cue used for 
planning though it might not make sense to an outside reader. 
This brings us to your outline. As you know from Chapter Two, an outline 
is a step ¢ professional development process. It’s a contracted stage of 
writing that generates “story by” credit and pays almost as much as a whole 
script. If Ä a contract with “cut-offs,” š get no further if the 
