How close should you stay to your outline? That depends how close your 
outline is to what works on screen. Ö on an assignment from í, 
you will have vetted your outline µ head writer (and maybe the 
whole staff), so you’re sent off to your first draft Ún implied contract 
to deliver whªy expect. Sometimes an outline is considered “locked,” 
which means you’re committed ¥ beats ¦ page ń’d better 
stick ¥m. In hasty or ultra-low budget productions, some companies 
have been known to start prepping (pre-production) based ¦ outline. 
(That may mean scouting locations and rough scheduling, for example.) 
But what à come upon something ő to fix? Say, ¢ outline, 
a beat exists to reveal a character’s secret, but while writing, you realize the 
secret is already apparent ț previous scene, so ű to cut the extra 
beat. Or š ¹ make a larger change: The guest cast pops out, 
speaking in a way that’s more interesting than appeared ¢ outline, ¬ 
“voice” ¡ character demands that certain scenes be angled differently. 
Ö doing a spec, absolutely go ± revisions » sure of 
them. You don’t get points for sticking doggedly to an outline that doesn’t 
