I’ll show you how easy it is to deliver in 14 days. Let’s say Ä an 
outline with 28 beats (four acts Úround seven scenes in each). I like 
to follow my outline exactly, so I write just two scenes each day. Voila! 14 
X 2 = 28. When I’m writing at home (not on staff), I begin each morning 
reading over what I wrote the day before, fine-tuning it. Then I take a 
breath and get ready for ¿ scene ¡ day. I approach it as if these 
next pages are the single most important piece of writing I’ll ever do. I 
¹ bring to this screen moment the richest experience, full of subtext 
and nuance, while delivering the action ¢ tightest way I can. I might 
imagine the whole scene before writing a word, or take a walk and jot 
down ideas, or close my eyes and wait for đ’ voices. Whatever 
it takes. Then I write two or three pages. And stop. 
I find that pushing on diminishes quality, and I ¹ come to ù 
scene fresh. So I’ll take a break. Lunch, errands, gym, calls — I try to take 
my mind off it, though when I’m most relaxed, not even trying. I’ll have 
© YOUR OWN E P I 5 0 D E I 4 9 
ideas for a way ï next scene or a perspective on one I wrote. Much 
later ¢ day, ¢ afternoon or night, I’ll re-read the morning’s scene 
and revise it. Then I repeª process of finding, forming, and writing the 
second scene ¡ day. And stop. 
I’ve found that Ü time I reach ² ē, my first draft has 
already been edited because I refine my work each day. Of course, not 
everyone works like this, nor should they. I have ą friend who starts 
work at 4:00 A.M. and smashes through as many pages as she can before 
she runs î steam, hardly looking back. She tells me she’d never let 
anyone see that “mess” that rambles, repeats, and wanders into tangents. 
She regards it as raw material which she edits away after she arrives ª 
end. You could think of it as the difference between painting and carving 
a sculpture: The painter pays attention to each brush stroke, adding one 
after another until the picture is formed. The sculptor begins Ú hunk 
of material, and cuts away “everything that isn’t the statue,” to para¬ 
phrase Michaelangelo. One method isn’t better than another — whatever 
works for you is right. 
Ö frightened by a blank page, put something on it — anything. A 
painting teacher once taught me that as I stood staring at a blank canvas. 
He walked over and threw ink on my pristine surface, and that got me 
moving, even if only to clean up the ink. Some writers break the empti¬ 
ness Úutomatic writing or anything a character might say, even if 
it’s not ú to open the scene. Some people write by hand ± 
visceral feel of words flowing À mind onto paper. Others talk into 
a tape recorder. 
Aaron Sorkin commented ¥ Writers Guild Foundation Seminar: 
