turning the pages, this is so much fun. Even now in ēs that I write, I only, 
frankly, describe what’s important for you × that moment. It’s possible that 
I’m ¶ describe, ‘¬ camera pushes in and pushes in and pushes in’ 
and I’m probably ¶ write it like that because I’m building tension ± 
reader at that point. Mostly I write for dialogue, and dialogue is ů read 
fastest ầ’re reading a screenplay. Description just slows it down...” 
Aim toward page 17 to end your first act (including the teaser if Ä 
one), page 30 to end Act Two, around 45 to end Act Three, and somewhere 
between 55 and 65 at ² Act Four (57 to 60 pages is an average 
total length). Those are approximate guides, not rules, though. A filmed 
episode might run anywhere from 44 to 52 minutes (without commercials), 
depending ¦ outlet, but you won’t know the actual length û script 
until several drafts from now when a shooting script is read through Ü 
cast and timed Ú stopwatch. I gave you that page count only so ¾ 
check yourself. Ö way off — for example an hour script that’s 30 pages 
