IF YOU'RE RUMMIMG LONG: 
• Are the speeches over-written, explanatory or redundant? Tighten the 
dialogue. 
• Have you indulged in set decoration, directing ¦ page, or over¬ 
blown description? Take a sharp knife ¥se. 
• Are certain acts long, though ē Î right length? Move the act 
breaks by enhancing a different cliffhanger or re-ordering scenes. 
• Have you indulged in backstory or secondary characters or tangents? 
Return to your original outline and stick to a clean, clear telling. 
• Have you engaged the scenes as close as possible ¥ conflict? Have 
you ended scenes immediately after the climax or goal? If Ä 
written prologues or epilogues to your scenes, get rid ¡m. 
• Is there too much story? ær outline was accurate, this shouldn’t be a 
problem, but š have fooled yourself ¢ outline by counting 
sequences of scenes as one beat. If so, ű to re-think ż 
themselves, or delete an entire arc. This is major work, not editing (see 
the discussion of “second draft”). 
