You ä underst¬ difference between revising and rewriting. The 
ô editing you do every day — fixing spelling and punctuation, tight¬ 
ening lines, omitting a speech, clarifying an action, lopping off the heads 
or tails of overlong scenes — all those corrections are parts of normal 
writing. Rewriting is a whole other job. 
A rewrite means re-thinking the structure and sometimes characters as 
well. You’re still dealing µ same general story, but ő a fresh 
ě tell it. You can’t do that by crossing out lines or replacing words. 
Go back ¥ drawing board. 
Start by putting aside ē. I mean it. As long as you cling ¥ 
precious moments you’ve written you’ll be tied to your first draft. Take a 
breath and let go. Maybe you’ll be able to use many ¡ pages you’ve 
written; and certain scenes, even sequences, might survive intact. But when 
you begin a rewrite, everything is ¦ table or you’ll turn into a pretzel 
İ fit a structure around scenes that don’t belong. 
Depending ¦ notes you got À producer or readers, your episode 
may require a new outline. Can you work with your existing outline as a 
reference to re-organize the beats, or do Ä to start over? Either way, 
boldly get rid of what hasn’t worked and add completely new elements, 
even a new arc, if necessary. 
Then beg¢ second draft using the new outline, though, again, š 
be able to keep much û first draft. Now, I’m not saying to throw out 
ð · bought (if indeed é bought). On that point — being 
told to do a second draft on an assignment is terrific news. The alternate 
is being cut off after ¿ draft and having your script given to another 
writer. Don’t imagine for a minute that anyone’s first draft is shot exactly as 
first written, not even when ·-runner writes it himself! And » 
on assignment, the second draft generates a payment. 
