to you as it does to me? Why didn’t I tighten that beat? No, é the 
director’s fault... or was it my fault?” But all that’s really fleeting. 
The more I considered what mattered in building a writing career I came 
to a single lesson: Make friends. No doubt, you’ve heard the line “it’s 
who you know, not ů know;” or, put another way, “this town is 
all about relationships.” Those glib sayings fit certain agents, managers, 
and ã. But I suggest you think about it somewhat differently as 
ą. 
Especially on TV series staffs, the act of creating õ private, though 
you certainly bring your unique talents. Writers tend to ¹ work 
with other writers who enable them to do their own best work. That often 
means choosing collaborators who make them comfortable å to 
take creative risks, and who can be trusted to deliver quality dialogue or 
story twists or humor or tales of life. Much of this rests on what’s ¦ 
page. But no producer-writer has the time to comb every writing sample. 
Producers hire whom they know. 
Now, that doesn’t mean Ä to party with powerful people or suck 
up ¥ir families. It means forming ê of professional trust. You 
do that through good work followed by staying in touch. Students just out 
of film school often form workshops that meet at each other’s apartments, 
not only for continuing feedback on writing, and commiseration, but also 
± connections. One of my writing students formed an alliance Ú 
producing student who ž be an agent. On graduating, the junior 
agent got a job as, well, a junior agent, and brought along the writer as a 
first client. In time, they rose together. 
Ö not in film school, š make similar connections at semi¬ 
nars, workshops, and extension classes open ¥ public. Or maybe 
you’ll land a beginning assignment on a small show. The people in 
the cubicles next to you aren’t always ¶ be in those cubicles. 
Someone’s ¶ move on to a better series, someone’s ¶ 
become a producer, someone’s ¶ be asked to recommend ą, 
maybe with qualifications just like yours. Join professional groups, and 
ầ’re eligible, become active ¢ Writers Guild. Even » 
shy or a hermit (or so focused on đ you’re creating you 
don’t ¹ be bothered Úctual humans), push yourself î 
your shell. That’s the one thing I wish I’d done more, and I offer it to 
you as the career lesson I learned. 
