If én’t for my history with one particular network executive I wouldn’t 
have been able to tell Đ that meant a lot to me. A teenage friÑ 
my daughter was visiting one day and mentioned, all too casually, that her 
mother had been diagnosed with breast cancer. The girl blew it off as if it 
didn’t affect her. I realized she was in deep denial, utterly unprepared to 
face the reality ¡ upcoming surgery. Thinking about her, it occurred 
to me that ³s had been done about breast cancer — ¬ last thing 
I ž do was a disease-of-the-week movie — but no one had dealt 
with this serious subject À daughter’s point of view. What inter¬ 
ested me was not the illness ấ relationship and how such an event 
would affect a teenager’s sense of what it means to be a woman, and what 
would happen if she lost her mother. 
Had I set out ½ ś, or even a treatment, or even a pitch, and 
asked my agent to arrange meetings with potential ã, followed 
by waiting ±ir responses, followed by scheduling network meetings, 
and re-scheduling them after they’re postponed, followed by who-knows- 
how-many network pitches, followed by who-knows-how-long-I’d-wait 
for an answer that ý no ... half a year might go by before I could 
write this, if ever. 
