A - B - C STORIES. Parallel plots within ę. Stories are denoted by 
letters (A, B, C, D...). Often each story follows the ­ arc of è 
the continuing cast. 
ABOVE-THE-LiniE. Production elements ª top of a budget breakdown, 
usually: ã, directors, writers, and sometimes composer or star. 
Crew positions are “below-the-line.” 
ACT. A ­ unit made of a number of scenes. Network TV hours 
usually have four acts that build to cliffhangers before commercial breaks, 
though some have five or six acts. Cable shows which do not have breaks 
may structure ° into three or four theoretical acts. 
ACTION. In ś, the description below a slug line that tells what is 
occurring on screen. Also see DESCRIPTION. 
AGENT. Someone who represents talent. A literary agent advances career 
opportunities for writers who are signed to him and usually negotiates his 
clients’ deals. Agents are licensed Ü state, and receive 10% commis¬ 
sions. 
ANTAGONIST. A character whose goal opposes the protagonist’s goal. 
Though sometimes considered the “villain,” in quality ³ the best 
opponent is a “worthy antagonist.” 
ANTHOLOGY. A show whose installments are free-standing and do not 
have continuing casts, such as Twilight Zone. 
ARC. The progression of a character from one condition to a different 
­ state. Example: A character who is cold ¢ beginning loves 
someone in Ð. 
A.T.A.S. Academy of Television Arts and Sciences. Membership organiza¬ 
tion for all fields of §. Produces the Emmy Awards, among other 
activities. 
250 
ATTACHED. Talent (for example, an actor) is “attached” when that person 
commits to participating in a proposed show. 
BACK MIME. The remaining nine ° in a traditional 22-week season 
after ¿ thirteen are finished. Even if ë is given a full-season 
order, the CREEM-LICHT to produce the back nine may depend on how well 
¿ thirteen perform. 
BACK STORY. History before the action on screen, usually incidents or 
relationships that shaped a character. 
BACK-UP SCRIPTS. Extra ° beyond ā that a network orders 
to assess í s potential when it õ given a slot ¦ air, some¬ 
times called back-up ®s. 
BACK DOOR PILOT. A two-hour movie that suggests potential for ë. 
beat. 1. A scene or step of ð; 2. A pause in dialogue or action. 
beat sheet. Also see outline. A list of events (turning points) ¢ 
story, often detailed in numbered scenes, though sometimes more gener¬ 
ally in sequences or even summaries of entire acts. 
BIBLE. A guide to â, especially for writers and directors joining 
í. Includes character biographies, rules for ă of ·, 
summaries of past °, and sometimes whª ã are seeking 
in tone, style or stories. 
BREAKING A STORY. Finding the major turning points (and often act 
breaks) in Đ before writing a detailed outline. 
CLIFFHANCER. A suspenseful ending; frequently jeopardy or raised stakes 
ª act break and end ¡ season. 
CLOSURE. In episodic ³, Đ that is complete (arrives at its goal or 
conclusion) at ² the hour. 
COLD OPENING. See TEASER. 
CONTINUING CAST. Main characters that return each week and “drive” the 
core stories in â. 
CREATIVE CONSULTANT. An experienced writer-producer who may oversee, 
re-write or write °, but might not be a full-time member ¡ 
staff. 
2 5 2 W H I I I N 6 I H E TV D H II M I) ¨ H M 0 0 U 6 L fl S 
DAILIES. Unedited scenes screened after each day of production. 
DEVELOPMENT. The process of bringing a project from concept to produc¬ 
tion; also the period when ą works with ã to refine ś 
through all revision steps. 
DESCRIPTION. The actions, images and sounds on screen, excluding 
dialogue. See ACTION. 
DIALOGUE. Everything đ say that may include expressions 
(i.e., sighs) and pauses as well as words. 
DRAMEDY. A hybrid of ³ and comedy. 
ELEMENT. Significant talent, such as a producer, director or actors that 
ò attached to a project to enhance its clout. 
EPISODE. In ­ §, a one-hour increment ¡ season; that 
week’s group of stories. Also an installment of a continuing story-line. 
EXECUTIVE PRODUCER. The top writer-producer on a TV series, often 
responsible for supervising both creative and business aspects of ·, 
overseeing the cast, crew and writing staff. See also SHOW-RUNNER. 
EXT. Abbreviation for Exterior, used in SLUG LINES. 
FIRST DRAFT. A script written Ü writer before responding to notes from 
a producer. All SPEC SCRIPTS and all drafts before submission are “first 
drafts,” no matter how many times the writer has privately made revisions. 
FORMAT. 1. A proposal for ë that may include characterizations, 
genre, SPRINGBOARDS for stories, and suggestions for ° or a PILOT. 
2. The structure of Đ, which may involve a FRANCHISE. 3. The printed 
style of ś, usually set by screenwriting software. 
FRANCHISE. Storytelling categories that generate ­ situations, such 
as medical, legal, or detective work. Successful series may also franchise 
their FORMAT, as in C.S.I. 
FREELANCE WRITER. A writer who õ on staff and receives an assign¬ 
ment for ę. 
GREEN LIGHT. Approval for ś to proceed to production. 
GUEST CAST. Actors not ¢ continuing cast who may appear in a 
limited number of °. 
6 I D S S II R Y 2 5 3 
HIATUS. The break between seasons when the entire staff is on vacation. 
HOOK. An early action or situation that grabs an ç’s attention; also 
an opening of a pitch. 
IN THE CAN. An episode that’s ready to air. 
INT. Abbreviation for Interior, used in slug lines. 
LEGS. A series with legs is capable of generating numerous stories for 
°. 
LOCKED. When a producer has approved the final version of an outline or 
script, it is locked. 
LOG LINE. A one sentence summary of Đ; occasionally ý two 
or three sentences. It quickly conveys the PREMISE ¬ ­ ARC 
or PLOT. 
LONG NARRATIVE. Stories that have the depth and range to continue for 
years, especially in serials. 
MANAGER. Similar to AGENT, except that managers are not licensed 
and may represent their clients in areas beyond immediate assignments. 
Managers typically charge 15%, compared Ú 10% commission for 
agents. 
M.B.A. Minimum Basic Agreement, the agreement between the Writers 
Guild of America and signatories (production entities) that specifies 
minimum pay, health and pension, and other contractual issues. 
OUTLINE. A list of every scene that will appear in ē, in order, 
beginning with number 1 in each act. Each outline “beat” is like a log line 
for that scene. However, some shows use outlines that are less specific. An 
outline is ¿ paid step in development. Also called STEP OUTLINE. 
See BEAT SHEET. 
OVERNIGHTS. Quick national ratings that give a preliminary account of 
