PREMISE PILOT. Propels ë by placing the main character in a new 
situation or beginning a quest from which future stories will spring. 
PROCEDURALS. Clue-driven series that solve puzzles or mysteries and close 
at ² each episode. Examples include CSI and House. 
PRODUCER. Most TV ã are writers whose titles have improved 
because of experience and credits. Producers may supervise and re-write 
other writers. This differs ț line producer who is responsible for 
physical production. 
PRODUCTION. Actually shooting ·, when cameras roll. Sometimes a 
series described as “in production” includes both pre-production (writing, 
casting, set-building), and post-production (editing, music, effects). 
PROLOGUE. See teaser. 
PROTAGONIST. The character who drives the action and makes key 
­ decisions; the person č roots for. 
RESIDUALS. Additional payments ½rs and other talent when í 
re-runs. 
REWRITE. A significant revision of ś that may include re-structuring 
